"{\"Claims\":[{\"id\":\"793412544\",\"title\":\"Can I have two separate brokerage commission lines for the same Broker?\",\"text\":\"This is possible for Freight Invoices as both lines will generate a brokerage commission each in the Description section of the Freight Invoice. However, for Demurrage Invoice adding the same broker twice in the Laytime Calculation (Claim Tab): i.e. Broker A 3.75% Broker A 1.25% is not supported by the system. As such, please consider the below two options: Aggregating the two into one single entry. If the above is not possible or not accepted by the Broker, it would be worth considering creating a \\\"dummy\\\" broker against which you can set the additional percentage in the Laytime Calculation screen (Claim Tab) which in turn will appear in the Demurrage Invoice.\"},{\"id\":\"681967795\",\"title\":\"How to prevent linked Demurrage/Despatch costs from aggregating on a Freight Invoice\",\"text\":\"Clients have the ability to control whether or not Demurrage/Despatch line items will aggregate or list independently in the PDF of the invoice with the Configuration Flag: CFGDisableItemizedDemOnFinalStatement There may be times when Demurrage/Despatch is linked to a Freight Invoice, and this flag is configured not to aggregate line items in the invoice (i.e., the flag is set to a value of 'N'), yet IMOS lists them together anyways with the verbiage: \\\"Demurrage/Despatch for all ports combined\\\" To correct this issue, follow the steps listed below. Example Instructions Navigate to Data Center > Settings > CFGDisableItemizedDemOnFinalStatement > Open the flag and ensure the value is set to 'N' > Click OK/Save Open Freight Invoice > Right-click and delete the afflicted Demurrage/Despatch line item > Save the Freight Invoice Create a new Laytime Calculation for the port > Set to statuses to 'Actual' and 'Settled'. Reopen the Freight Invoice > Click the \\\"Add Details\\\" button in the taskbar > Check the box for the new invoice > Click Ok Save the invoice. Click the Invoice button on the taskbar and you should now see the correct formatting (i.e., it will no longer read \\\"Demurrage for all ports combined\\\") If necessary, reverse and delete the previous and afflicted laytime invoice.\"},{\"id\":\"2014740692\",\"title\":\"Why am I getting the program message,\\\"Error in update: Table task has been updated since last read, record cannot be saved.\\\"\",\"text\":\"This error typically occurs when multiple users are making updates simultaneously. It can also happen if updates are being made in multiple imos tabs at the same time. To prevent this error: Use only one imos tab when making updates. Refresh and save the page before proceeding with any changes. Following these steps usually resolves the issue.\"},{\"id\":\"1912996009\",\"title\":\"Why do the fields Total Demurrage Time, Demurrage Days, and Demurrage rate not show on the Laytime Calculation Report?\",\"text\":\"The Report is accessed from the Laytime Calculation screen, select &lsquo;Reports' > &lsquo;Calculation Report'. These fields are linked to the different Laytime Calculation methods. If the Calculation method is &lsquo;Standard' these fields will not show on the Laytime Calculation Report. If the Calculation method is &lsquo;Reversible' these fields will show on the Laytime Calculation Report. There is more information available on the different Calculation methods on the following page: VIP - Laytime Calculation\"},{\"id\":\"1953792005\",\"title\":\"Why is the broker commission on a demurrage invoice being calculated on net demurrage instead of total demurrage?\",\"text\":\"If the demurrage and despatch are together, IMOS is going to factor the calculation as demurrage less despatch. To avoid this, we suggest creating one laytime claim for the port containing all the despatch and another claim for the demurrage. On the demurrage calculation's claim tab, check the address commission included, while on the despatch calculation, do not select the check box.\"},{\"id\":\"868122691\",\"title\":\"Why port activities are not imported into laytime calculation?\",\"text\":\"Upon submitting Statement of Facts for a given port, the expected is to create a laytime calculation to claim demurrage in a timely manner. Typically, the port activities are imported automatically by choosing \\\"Import Activities\\\" e.g. from Voyage. Issue: Not all the port activities were imported e.g. Start / End Discharging Cargo into laytime calculation, although they are visible in the Port Activities for a given discharge port. Solution: Check if a specific port activity is marked as the one to be imported into laytime calculation. Go to Data Center > Port Activities > make sure Laytime Calc Import is ticked. [image] In case of activities like Start Loading or Discharging cargo, check if they are linked with the cargo. In the BL Info tab of Voyage Itinerary, it should state the cargo name and BL Code on the load/discharge port leg and so the same should be reflected in the Port Activities populated by SOF form. [image] BL code is populated as soon as cargo name is entered.\"}],\"Configuration Flags\":[{\"id\":\"868778194\",\"title\":\"CFGChartererView\",\"text\":\"FAQs > Configuration Flags > CFGChartererView This article provides the following information about this configuration flag : Name Charterer View Description\"},{\"id\":\"868778179\",\"title\":\"CFGEnableCargoItinSupplierReceiver\",\"text\":\"FAQs > Configuration Flags > CFGEnableCargoItinSupplierReceiver This article provides the following information about this configuration flag : Name Enable Cargo Itinerary Supplier Receiver Description\"},{\"id\":\"1857290338\",\"title\":\"CFGEnableExtraFrtRateScaleTable\",\"text\":\"[image] Requires CFGEnableExtraFrtRateScaleTable to be enabled In the Cargo Contract's Extra Freight Terms grid, when a scale table value is populated, then the right-click dropdown menu will display two new values: Choose Scale Table - When selected the scale table selector modal will pop up where the user can choose the specific scale table associated with this line item (NOTE: This is what is currently linked to Edit Scale table) Edit Scale Table - When selected the Rate Table definition for the selected scale table will open and the user can edit the specific scale table. If the user does not have security permission to edit the Rate Tables then a warning will trigger letting them know that they don't have permissions (same warning as elsewhere in the system) In the COA Contract's Extra Freight Terms grid, when a scale table value is populated, then the right-click dropdown menu will display two new values: Choose Scale Table - When selected the scale table selector modal will pop up where the user can choose the specific scale table associated with this line item (NOTE: This is what is currently linked to Edit Scale table) Edit Scale Table - When selected the Rate Table definition for the selected scale table will open and the user can edit the specific scale table. If the user does not have security permission to edit the Rate Tables then a warning will trigger letting them know that they don't have permissions (same warning as elsewhere in the system) When the Edit Scale Table option is selected, the user can also create a New Scale table by clicking the '+' icon [image]\"},{\"id\":\"737149387\",\"title\":\"CFGIgnoreAccrualOnVoyageDelete\",\"text\":\"FAQs > Configuration Flags > CFGIgnoreAccrualOnVoyageDelete This article provides the following information about this configuration flag : Name Ignore Accrual on Voyage Delete Description Use Case This will occur when accruals are set to always prorate and users schedule out voyages with P&L actuals well in advance. If adjustments need to be made to the vessel or voyage and accruals have run, you can either: Follow the below steps to bypass the error that shows when adjustments are attempted Enable CFGIgnoreAccrualOnVoyageDelete You will then see the following warning instead: [image] To get around the financial transaction error when adjusting vessel/voyage details on a voyage with an accrual (without the flag): Rerun accruals on a recalculation basis for the specific vessel/voyage. This will refresh those two Pending status records, and they will be available for rejection. Once records are rejected, you can delete the voyage, update the cargo, update the voyage itinerary, etc.\"},{\"id\":\"249954319\",\"title\":\"CFGTCOBunkerAdj\",\"text\":\"FAQs > Configuration Flags > CFGTCOBunkerAdj This article provides the following information about this configuration flag : Name TCO Bunker Adjustment Description Use Case The default system setting of configuration flag CFGTCOBunkerAdj is N (disabled). If it is disabled, there will be no TCO bunker adjustment values. TCO bunker delivery and redelivery prices invoiced from TCO hire statements will only affect your bunker inventory (balance sheet account: asset account) alongside AP/AR pair. When CFGTCOBunkerAdj is set to Y (enabled), the gain/loss from delivery and redelivery bunkers are recorded. For more information, see How is TCO bunker adjustment calculated? . When generating Voyage Period Journals , there will be an extra journal, TCO Bunker Adjustment . The calculated price difference can be set to be linked to a P&L-affecting account (expense account), thus allocating (debit/credit) money from your bunker inventory asset account to recognize gain/loss from TCO delivery bunker prices. Will this adjustment value go away when the same amount and price of bunkers are redelivered for the TCO contract? No, this only happens during TCO bunker delivery. If your inventory price and TCO bunker delivery price is the same, there will be no adjustment. When the bunkers are being redelivered, the vessel's bunker inventory price will be updated according to the price of the TCO bunkers. How do I remove a bunker adjustment for a singular voyage? To remove a bunker adjustment from the P&L the inventory price must be equal to the bunker delivery price.\"},{\"id\":\"510329108\",\"title\":\"CFGUseNaturalRounding\",\"text\":\"FAQs > Configuration Flags > CFGUseNaturalRounding This article provides the following information about this configuration flag : Name Use Natural Rounding Description Alternative Method for Invoice Rounding You may encounter one-cent rounding differences in invoices and claims. This is because binary floating-point arithmetic is used to perform quick calculations in form displays. This occasionally results in one-cent differences in values displayed in invoices, even though the invoice total shows the correct amount. For example, a claim has a 1.25% commission on the total amount of $987.60. Claim amount : 987.60 * 1.25% = 12.345 &asymp; 12.35 However, the invoice display may show a value of 12.34 instead of 12.35 . You may choose to use an alternative rounding method throughout the platform by enabling the CFGUseNaturalRounding configuration flag. Notes : The configuration flag change will not affect posted invoices. It will apply to new invoices only. For clients using IMOS: You must restart IMOS after applying the changes. This flag may not appear in the Configuration Flag List ; administrators may need to click the Custom Flag button and enter the flag name to open and modify its value.\"}],\"Cross-Platform\":[{\"id\":\"5278760989\",\"title\":\"How do I allow access to the Veson Platform in IMOS?\",\"text\":\"All IMOS clients now have access to the Veson Platform, including enhanced IMOS workflows, CoCaptain, Mail, and Insights. Admins are enabled first and can then grant access to their users. For a Specific User Navigate to IMOS > Data Center > Security and go to a specific user profile. Select Allow Veson Platform Access on that user's profile and Save . [image] For All Users In IMOS > Settings, select the Grid icon at the top of the screen, under Configuration Flag List. [image] Select Allow Veson Platform Access, under Veslink Site Info, and save. [image] Accessing the Platform Once you've made these changes, go to app.veson.com to access the platform. Users can sign in with their existing IMOS credentials.\"},{\"id\":\"5261623305\",\"title\":\"How do I chat with CoCaptain?\",\"text\":\"CoCaptain is Veson's AI assistant. It's part of the Veson Platform , Veson's unified, AI-first experience that brings together IMOS, CoCaptain, Mail, and Veson Insights. Already working inside the Veson Platform? You already have full CoCaptain access &mdash; head straight to the CoCaptain article for complete guidance. The following steps are for clients still using IMOS as a standalone product. What is CoCaptain? CoCaptain is the contextual AI layer built into the Veson Platform. It's designed to work directly with your live IMOS data &mdash; so you can ask questions about voyages, cargoes, invoices, and counterparties, chat in the context of a specific record you're viewing, run analysis, and even update records, all through natural conversation. How does CoCaptain compare to Fin? Fin and CoCaptain are both part of your AI experience &mdash; just suited to different moments in your workflow. Fin is built into the IMOS experience you use every day, ready to answer \\\"how do I\\\" questions right where you're working. CoCaptain is the deeper AI layer available through the enhanced Veson Platform experience of IMOS &mdash; the same IMOS, now with more AI built in. Where it lives: Fin is available in your current IMOS experience as well as other Veson products. CoCaptain is available in the Veson Platform experience of IMOS, currently accessed at app.veson.com with your existing IMOS credentials &mdash; no new account to set up. What it helps with: Fin helps with everyday \\\"how do I\\\" questions. CoCaptain goes further, pulling in the specifics of a voyage, cargo, or counterparty directly from IMOS so you can dig into the details. Do I already have access? If you're a current IMOS client, CoCaptain is included at no additional cost. Your existing IMOS credentials work. If you try to open CoCaptain and see a message that you don't have access to that area, this is usually a quick permissions fix &mdash; see Enabling Veson Platform Access for Users for the steps. A small number of environments have specific security or configuration requirements that mean Veson Platform access isn't available yet. If that applies to you, your Account Manager will already be in touch &mdash; reach out to them directly with any questions. If you're not currently an IMOS client, CoCaptain isn't included in your subscription today. Reach out to your Account Manager to discuss access. How can I log in? Go to app.veson.com and sign in with your existing IMOS credentials &mdash; no new account or password needed. Still need help? If you have access enabled but run into trouble logging in, or your administrator can't locate the Veson Platform access setting, please reach out to Veson Support and we'll help you get connected.\"},{\"id\":\"5271093298\",\"title\":\"What is the Vessel Insights field in IMOS?\",\"text\":\"This field is solely for Veson Platform users who use Vessel Insights in that product. It's a new column in the IMOS Data Center > Vessels > Fuel/Lube Types table that lets you map a Vessel Insights fuel type (HFO, LFO, or MDO) to a corresponding IMOS fuel type, such as IFO, LSFO, ULSFO, LSMGO, or BIO. This mapping tells IMOS which of your existing fuel types to use when it ingests vessel consumption profile data supplied by Vessel Insights. I don't use Veson Platform or Vessel Insights &mdash; why do I see this field in IMOS? The field is part of the standard IMOS Fuel/Lube Types table, so it appears for all IMOS users regardless of whether your organization uses Veson Platform. If you don't use Vessel Insights, you can leave it unmapped &mdash; it has no effect on your existing workflows. Do I need to fill this in if I don't use Vessel Insights? No. Leaving the field blank does not affect your fuel/lube type configuration, vessel data, or any other IMOS functionality. Does this change anything about how Fuel/Lube Types work otherwise? No. This adds a new mapping column to the existing Fuel/Lube Types table; it doesn't change how fuel/lube types are created, edited, or used elsewhere in IMOS.\"}]}"